2026-08-01 22:55:14 | INFO | Pre-registered control (c), in two halves that see different failures. HALF 1, per-arm instability: the 36 held-out counts are compared to their own calibration counts by a conditional two-sample test, which removes the unknown true level of each threshold from the analysis, and the KS statistic of those p-values against Uniform(0,1) gates at p > 0.01. This half is blind to a bias common to every arm, since both counts would move together. HALF 2, common bias: the pooled held-out level must intersect [0.047000, 0.053000], the band the procedure promises, its half-width being the bisection tolerance 0.003 ALONE. The tolerance is systematic and does not shrink under pooling; the sampling uncertainty is carried by the interval instead, with the factor 2 above, and counting it in both places would double-count it. Both terms derive from 0.05, 0.003, 2000 and 36 alone, with no regenerated value read.
2026-08-01 22:55:14 | INFO | Pre-registered control (c), reported and NOT gating: the literal predicate of the prompt, 'the pooled interval contains 0.05', and the KS statistic of the 36 one-sample binomial p-values against Uniform(0,1). The latter tests the null that every arm sits at exactly 0.05, which the bisection never promises and the factor above shows it cannot deliver: that test omits half the variance of its own statistic and therefore fires by construction rather than by accident of the draw.
